{"notes":[{"content":{"decision":{"value":"Accept"},"comment":{"value":"This work proposes GraphWalker, an agentic KGQA framework that leverages synthetic trajectory generation and a stage-wise curriculum (GraphSynth: random walk trajectories -> GraphRoll: distilled expert trajectories -> RL) to improve exploration and reasoning over large knowledge graphs. The papers shows that GraphWalker achieves consistent empirical gains across multiple benchmarks (CWQ, WebQSP, GrailQA) and extensive ablations (e.g., impact of each training stage and key components of the proposed data pipeline).\n\n### Positive Aspects\n\n- A formulation that moves beyond static subgraph retrieval toward global KG interaction, which is a more general and realistic setting for KGQA.\n\n- A simple yet effective curriculum design for agent training for large knowledge graph exploration. It leverages synthetic data generation and distillation to mitigate the challenges of training from scratch with RL.\n\n- Consistent empirical gains across multiple benchmarks (CWQ, WebQSP, GrailQA). It also introduces a new benchmark (GraphWalkerBench) to further validate the approach.\n\n- Rich experimental analysis, including extensive ablations (e.g., impact of each training stage and key components of the proposed data pipeline, pass@k analysis to support the better exploration claim).\n\n\n### Identified Weaknesses\n\n- CWQ and WebQSP are old and relatively saturated KGQA benchmarks (e.g., recent models are close to 90%+ accuracy), which limits the strength of the empirical evaluation. The authors partially address this by adding results on GrailQA and a new benchmark (GraphWalkerBench).\n\n- Potential data leakage or weak “zero-shot” claims due to schema exposure during training. The authors address this by adding a controlled experiment (GraphWalker-CWQ) and reframing the claim as cross-benchmark generalization.\n\n- Baseline comparability and fairness. The authors clarify the grounding and global KG access of GraphWalker, and add a new baseline (GraphRetriever) to better isolate the contribution of the agentic formulation."},"title":{"value":"Paper Decision"}},"parentInvitations":"colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/-/Decision","nonreaders":[],"tmdate":1787537454656,"tcdate":1783524406580,"writers":["colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference","colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/Program_Chairs"],"signatures":["colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/Program_Chairs"],"forum":"x736SkmjeO","number":1,"license":"CC BY 4.0","cdate":1783524406580,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/Submission1979/-/Decision","colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1787537454656,"domain":"colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference","replyto":"x736SkmjeO","id":"iZ8Jv9pTTn","forumContent":{"TLDR":{"value":"GraphWalker trains a KGQA agent via synthetic random-walk trajectories and stage-wise curriculum fine-tuning, then refines it with lightweight RL to achieve state-of-the-art performance."},"venue":{"value":"COLM 2026"},"code_of_ethics":{"value":"I acknowledge that I and all co-authors of this work have read and commit to adhering to the COLM Code of Ethics on https://colmweb.org/CoE.html"},"LLM_usage":{"value":"In accordance with the Policy on the use of Large Language Models in the Call for Papers https://colmweb.org/cfp.html, I certify that my submission discloses any substantive use of LLMs in the research and paper-writing process."},"keywords":{"value":["LLM","Agent","Knowledge Graph Question Answering"]},"abstract":{"value":"Agentic knowledge graph question answering (KGQA) requires an agent to iteratively interact with knowledge graphs (KGs), posing challenges in both training data scarcity and reasoning generalization. Specifically, existing approaches often restrict agent exploration: prompting-based methods lack autonomous navigation training, while current training pipelines usually confine reasoning to predefined trajectories. To this end, this paper proposes GraphWalker, a novel agentic KGQA framework that addresses these challenges through Automated Trajectory Synthesis and Stage-wise Fine-tuning. GraphWalker adopts a two-stage SFT training paradigm: First, the agent is trained on structurally diverse trajectories synthesized from constrained random-walk paths, establishing a broad exploration prior over the KG; Second, the agent is further fine-tuned on a small set of expert trajectories to develop reflection and error recovery capabilities. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our stage-wise SFT paradigm unlocks a higher performance ceiling for a lightweight reinforcement learning (RL) stage, enabling GraphWalker to achieve state-of-the-art performance on CWQ and WebQSP. Additional results on GrailQA and our constructed GraphWalkerBench confirm that GraphWalker enhances generalization to out-of-distribution reasoning paths. The code is publicly available at https://github.com/XuShuwenn/GraphWalker."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nxu2026graphwalker,\ntitle={GraphWalker: Agentic Knowledge Graph Question Answering via Synthetic Trajectory Curriculum},\nauthor={Shuwen Xu and Yao Xu and Jiaxiang Liu and Chenhao Yuan and Wenshuo Peng and Jun Zhao and Kang Liu},\nbooktitle={Third Conference on Language Modeling},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=x736SkmjeO}\n}"},"title":{"value":"GraphWalker: Agentic Knowledge Graph Question Answering via Synthetic Trajectory Curriculum"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/271907409c3d998af6480edddeba4a8bff53bdf9.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference"},"paperhash":{"value":"xu|graphwalker_agentic_knowledge_graph_question_answering_via_synthetic_trajectory_curriculum"},"authorids":{"value":["~Shuwen_Xu1","~Yao_Xu3","~Jiaxiang_Liu5","~Chenhao_Yuan3","~Wenshuo_Peng1","~Jun_Zhao4","~Kang_Liu1"]},"author_guide":{"value":"I certify that this submission complies with the submission instructions as described on https://colmweb.org/AuthorGuide.html"},"authors":{"value